Z||000014||87GTZ34||11-30-2001||10:33 AM||||1FST,  the description of the symptom is a 'current limited circuit'.  That means that something in the circuit itself is limiting the current not the load, and when several devices share a source and/or a ground, one can steal from another.  Usually, the bigger load steals from the smaller load (just like in real life).  I looked in the GM manual and sure enough the horn, radio/clock and interior courtesy lights (not dome though) all run off of the same source.  So it sounds like either the ground or the source is limiting current so when the courtesy lights go on, they steal ground or source (and thus current) from the radio.  Also your horn may not be getting enough current to operate but provides more source or ground (and thus current) making the lights brighter.  The fuse as mentioned may be limiting current or it could be bad ground(s) or even a bad connection going into the box (wires, connector or 'hot at all times' fusible link).  To test this theory b4 changing anything, remove the courtesty lamps (but not the dome) and run your open door/radio preset test.<BR>  This is also the basis for the side marker light operation.  In case anyone is wondering about that, do this test: put a turn signal on and watch the behavior of the front indicator and that corner's side marker light.  In sync correct, now keeping the signal on, turn the park lights on and check 'em out... <P>edited do to opps of the fusible link<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 11-30-2001).]||||reg||1
